  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nHigh pressure extending from Ontario to the southern Plains\ncontinues to dominate the weather across central Illinois\ntoday...resulting in sunny skies with above normal temperatures\nin the lower to middle 70s. As the ridge axis begins to nudge\neastward, a light E/SE return flow will develop tonight. \nDespite the increasingly southerly wind component, clear skies \nand a very dry boundary layer will allow excellent radiational\ncooling to occur as overnight lows drop into the middle 40s.\nWith full sunshine and surface winds veering to S/SW, highs on\nTuesday will climb into the middle to upper 70s. Even warmer \nconditions are expected on Wednesday ahead of an approaching \ncold front as readings soar into the lower to middle 80s.\n\nGiven the lack of deep-layer moisture, the weak cold front will\ndrop through the region virtually unnoticed...except for perhaps\na few clouds and a shift in the wind. Highs on Thursday and \nFriday behind the front will range from the lower 70s north of \nthe Peoria area to the lower 80s south of I-70. As upper ridging\nbuilds over the Midwest, the frontal boundary will get shunted\nback northward on Friday. Previous models had shown a few\nshowers along the front Thursday night into Friday: however, the\n12z Oct 5 suite suggests any precip will remain well N/NW of\ncentral Illinois. Once the front surges back northward, a warm\nand very dry weekend will be on tap with highs in the 80s.\n\nGiven the prolonged stretch of dry weather and relative humidity\nvalues routinely dropping to 30% or less during the afternoons,\na heightened risk for agricultural field fires exists through \nat least Saturday. After that, boundary layer moisture will be\non the increase by early next week as tropical moisture flows\nnorthward from the Gulf. Current satellite imagery shows a\ndisturbance off the coast of the Yucatan Peninsula that NHC\ngives a 70% chance of development over the next 7 days. Synoptic\nmodels all show the system lifting northward toward the Gulf\nCoast by the weekend...but with very different depictions of how\nfar N/NW its moisture will spread. The GFS continues to be the\nstrongest with the tropical system and furthest west with its \nmoisture plume...reaching central Illinois by Sunday. Meanwhile \nthe ECMWF is weaker and keeps the tropical moisture mostly E/SE \nin the Tennessee and Ohio River Valleys. At this time range, too\nmuch uncertainty exists to pin down the exact track of the \nmoisture and any potential rain chances. The 12z NBM brings 20 \nPoPs as far north as I-70 Sunday/Sunday night...with 20 PoPs \nacross all of central Illinois by Monday.",
